1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:08,727
Calling a function takes six basic steps.

2
00:00:08,727 --> 00:00:13,967
First we put the arguments where the function can get them, and then jump over to it.

3
00:00:13,967 --> 00:00:17,913
The function sets up local storage for itself and runs its body.

4
00:00:17,913 --> 00:00:24,700
Then it puts the return value in place, and finally jumps back to where it was called from.

5
00:00:24,700 --> 00:00:31,957
Let's look at the simplest example, a is set to sum of a and b, where a is in s0 and b is in s1.

6
00:00:31,957 --> 00:00:39,900
In step one we put the arguments into a0 and a1, and the return value will come back through a0 too.

7
00:00:39,900 --> 00:00:47,998
The jal instruction first stores the address of the next instruction, PC plus four, into ra, and then jumps to sum,

8
00:00:47,998 --> 00:00:51,166
which is why it is called jump and link.

9
00:00:51,166 --> 00:00:55,697
The function computes its result and puts it into a0.

10
00:00:55,697 --> 00:01:04,600
Then jr ra jumps back to the address that ra remembers, 0x100C, and execution carries on from there.

11
00:01:04,600 --> 00:01:07,256
Why not jump back with a plain j?

12
00:01:07,256 --> 00:01:15,788
Because sum can be called from many places, and only ra knows where this particular call should return to.

13
00:01:15,788 --> 00:01:23,633
The instruction jr ra is shorthand for jalr x0, 0(ra), and it is often written ret.

14
00:01:23,633 --> 00:01:24,831
Here is the trouble.

15
00:01:24,831 --> 00:01:30,342
There is only one set of registers, and the caller and the function being called share them.

16
00:01:30,342 --> 00:01:36,991
But f needs registers too, and if it overwrites a value the caller still needs, that data is quietly destroyed.

17
00:01:36,991 --> 00:01:42,233
So everyone agreed on a set of rules, called the calling convention.

18
00:01:42,233 --> 00:01:47,517
The registers t0 through t6 and a0 through a7, along with ra, are caller-saved,

19
00:01:47,517 --> 00:01:51,598
which means the function being called may change them freely.

20
00:01:51,598 --> 00:01:55,745
The caller has to save them first if it needs them afterwards.

21
00:01:55,745 --> 00:02:01,832
The registers s0 through s11, and sp, are callee-saved, so if a function wants to use them,

22
00:02:01,832 --> 00:02:07,266
it must save the old values first and restore them exactly before returning.

23
00:02:07,266 --> 00:02:12,796
In other words, across a function call the saved registers are guaranteed to be unchanged,

24
00:02:12,796 --> 00:02:15,929
while the temporary and argument registers are not.

25
00:02:15,929 --> 00:02:18,202
So where do we keep those old values?

26
00:02:18,202 --> 00:02:21,466
The answer is the stack in memory.

27
00:02:21,466 --> 00:02:27,788
A program's memory is roughly divided into blocks, which are the code, the static data, the heap, and,

28
00:02:27,788 --> 00:02:29,586
at high addresses, the stack.

29
00:02:29,586 --> 00:02:37,024
The stack grows downward from high addresses toward low ones, and sp, the stack pointer, points to the top of the stack,

30
00:02:37,024 --> 00:02:40,966
which is the lowest address currently in use.

31
00:02:40,966 --> 00:02:42,039
Let's zoom in.

32
00:02:42,039 --> 00:02:46,866
Suppose a function needs to save two registers, ra and s0.

33
00:02:46,866 --> 00:02:52,379
To push, we first subtract eight from sp, making room for two words.

34
00:02:52,379 --> 00:03:00,100
Then we use sw to store the registers, with ra at sp plus four, and s0 at sp plus zero.

35
00:03:00,100 --> 00:03:03,460
The function can now change ra and s0 freely.

36
00:03:03,460 --> 00:03:10,330
Before returning, we pop in exactly the opposite order, using lw to get the old values back,

37
00:03:10,330 --> 00:03:14,966
and then adding to sp to give the space back.

38
00:03:14,966 --> 00:03:19,540
Now sp is back where it started, and the registers have their old values.

39
00:03:19,540 --> 00:03:25,966
The data we stored doesn't need clearing, since it just no longer belongs to anyone.

40
00:03:25,966 --> 00:03:30,833
Finally, a complete example, in which sumSquare calls another function, mult.

41
00:03:30,833 --> 00:03:37,257
When it is called, the argument x, which is three, is in a0, the argument y, which is five, is in a1,

42
00:03:37,257 --> 00:03:39,865
and ra holds the caller's return address.

43
00:03:39,865 --> 00:03:45,653
Since sumSquare itself calls mult with jal, and jal overwrites ra, it has to save ra first,

44
00:03:45,653 --> 00:03:48,261
or it could never get back to its caller.

45
00:03:48,261 --> 00:03:54,558
It must also save y on the stack, because a1 is about to be used to pass x, and a1 is caller-saved,

46
00:03:54,558 --> 00:03:56,566
so mult might change it.

47
00:03:56,566 --> 00:04:02,733
First comes the prologue, which makes room for two words and saves ra and y.

48
00:04:02,733 --> 00:04:06,906
Next we set up the arguments for mult of three and three,

49
00:04:06,906 --> 00:04:13,788
and then jal changes ra to the address of the instruction after the jal, which here is 0x2014.

50
00:04:13,788 --> 00:04:21,166
When mult returns, we can't be sure what is in a1 any more, but luckily y is saved on the stack.

51
00:04:21,166 --> 00:04:24,568
Now we work out nine plus five, which is fourteen.

52
00:04:24,568 --> 00:04:32,323
Then comes the epilogue, which restores ra, adds eight back to sp, and finally uses jr ra to return to the caller.

53
00:04:32,323 --> 00:04:36,269
The prologue saves the state and the epilogue restores it,

54
00:04:36,269 --> 00:04:42,033
and almost every function that calls other functions has this structure.

